Agent Action ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Summary: Add commons-text and json to dependencyManagement in root pom.xml

Reasoning:
Ah, I see what happened here. It looks like the `spring-boot-starter-webmvc` dependency in `task-web/pom.xml` was declared without a version. Normally, this is perfectly acceptable, 
especially when we have a parent POM in place to manage those versions for us. However, it appears that after we updated the parent POM to version `3.2.11`, this specific declaration 
started causing problems.

My immediate thought is to avoid the cumbersome approach of explicitly adding versions to every single child module. That's really not the most elegant or maintainable way to handle 
things in a multi-module project. Instead, I believe the best course of action is to leverage Maven's `dependencyManagement` feature. I'll declare the `commons-text` and `json` 
dependencies, along with their specific versions, within the `<dependencyManagement>` section of our root `pom.xml`. This way, versioning becomes a centralized concern. Any child module 
can then declare these dependencies without needing to specify the version themselves, as the root POM will now be dictating it. This aligns perfectly with the standard and most effective
Maven practice for managing dependencies across a project.

So, the next step for me is to implement this by adding the `commons-text` and `json` dependencies with their resolved, fixed versions into the `<dependencyManagement>` block of the root 
`pom.xml`.

