Summary: Attempt to download spring-boot-starter-webmvc:3.2.10 from Spring Snapshots repository

Reasoning:
Okay, so after that last OSV scan, the fact that it came back with an empty `results` array once I commented out the `task-web` module is a pretty strong indicator. It really suggests 
that the issue with `commons-lang3:3.13.0` was either a false positive all along, or Maven's dependency resolution was handling it behind the scenes, but the OSV scanner was struggling to
parse the entire project correctly.

Now that the other parts of the project are building clean and the scanner is quiet without `task-web`, my immediate priority has to be getting `task-web` to behave.

The persistent blocker here, as I see it, is that `spring-boot-starter-webmvc:3.2.10` just won't resolve.

I've already gone through the usual suspects:
*   I tried rolling back to `3.2.10` of Spring Boot.
*   I made sure both the `central` and `spring-milestones` repositories were explicitly defined in the POM.
*   I've thoroughly cleared out my local Maven cache.

Looking at the error message from the last attempt, it's quite explicit:
`org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-webmvc:jar:3.2.10 was not found in https://repo.maven.apache.org/maven2 during a previous attempt. This failure was cached in the local 
repository and resolution is not reattempted until the update interval of central has elapsed or updates are forced`
`Could not find artifact org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-webmvc:jar:3.2.10 in spring-milestones (https://repo.spring.io/milestone)`

This leaves me with very little doubt: `3.2.10` is genuinely not showing up in *either* the main Maven Central repository or the Spring Milestones repository. It's either absent or 
there's some persistent network hiccup preventing me from seeing it.

So, my next step needs to be a direct verification of its availability. As a last-ditch effort, I'm going to try `mvn dependency:get` targeting the `spring-snapshots` repository, even 
though this is supposed to be a release version. It’s a long shot, but sometimes internal redirects or mirror configurations can lead to unexpected resolution paths.

If that still fails to pull the artifact, my next course of action will be to revisit changing the `spring-boot-starter-webmvc` version. This time, however, I will specifically target 
`3.2.11` and *crucially*, I will double-check that `3.2.11` is actually present and accessible within the `spring-milestones` repository before attempting the build.

For the immediate moment, I'm going to uncomment the `task-web` module again and then run that `mvn dependency:get` with `spring-snapshots`.

